Caveat's in and working. Both variants are above: pure Caveat, then the hybrid.

## How the install works

Tailwind v4 is CSS-first, so there's no config file — `@font-face` and `@theme` both live in `global.css`:

```css
@font-face {
  font-family: 'Caveat';
  font-weight: 400 700;        /* variable axis, one file covers the range */
  font-display: swap;
  src: url('../fonts/caveat-latin.woff2') format('woff2');
}
@theme { --font-hand: 'Caveat', ui-rounded, cursive; }
```

`@theme` generates the `font-hand` utility, which the poster SVG wears; SVG text inherits it. Self-hosted rather than the Google CDN — no third-party request, works offline, and no CDN dependency for a static artifact. I pulled the **Latin subset only** (76 KB) since the full family carries Cyrillic ranges this poster will never set, and `OFL.txt` sits beside it because distributing the file obliges shipping the licence.

`textWidth()` now reads the stack from the `--font-hand` theme variable rather than `document.body`, so CSS stays the single source of truth, and `useFontsReady()` re-renders once `document.fonts.ready` resolves — the FOUT problem I predicted, made real. Without it, chips measured during the swap window would be sized to the fallback font permanently.

## Two things you should weigh

**Legibility, as you feared, is real but localised.** Caveat has a small x-height for its em, so it sets visibly smaller than a sans at the same nominal size — I bumped `LABEL_FONT` 12 → 14 to compensate. Crop names survive that fine. The month axis doesn't: at 11 units in a script face, `Jan`/`Jun`/`Jul` are genuinely harder to separate, and that axis is the instrument everything else is read against. In the hybrid I left it in `font-sans`. That also lines up with the OSU extension guidance — clean sans where it has to be read, character where it can afford to be.

**Bun inlines the font as base64 into the CSS.** This is the part I'd flag hardest. The CSS chunk went from a few KB to **136 KB raw / 84 KB gzipped**, essentially all font, and it sits in the render-blocking stylesheet. Total payload goes ~192 KB → ~270 KB gzipped, a 40% increase on a project where you deliberately cut 63 KB of zod and 44 KB of motion.

I tried to avoid it — `loader: { '.woff2': 'file' }` doesn't apply (that map governs JS imports, not CSS `url()`), and an absolute `/fonts/...` URL fails the build outright because Bun resolves those too. There's no escape hatch through Bun's CSS pipeline that I could find.

It's not purely a cost: inlining means the font is present the moment CSS parses, so there's no FOUT at all and `useFontsReady` becomes belt-and-braces. But you're paying ~78 KB of render-blocking bytes for it, and the font can no longer be cached independently of the CSS. If that trade bothers you, the way out is loading it via the `FontFace` JS API from a file copied verbatim into `dist` — more moving parts, and it reintroduces the swap window the hook already handles.

Everything's unstaged, including the new `src/fonts/`. CI green.

Multi-value OR works — three districts in one request, 1356 → 213 rows.

**~3 added lines, ~4 modified.** Well under your threshold:

```ts
// client.ts — pull()
+ const district = src.districts.map((d) => encodeURIComponent(`"${d}"`)).join(',');
~ const query = `commodity=${commodity};district=${district};report_begin_date=${from}:${to}`;
+ districts: src.districts,        // into CacheMeta, so the cache records what it excluded
// format.ts
+ districts: string[];             // CacheMeta
// header comment, and MarsSource's doc: "prefix" → exact names
```

The only real edit beyond that is `DISTRICTS` becoming exact names (`'SALINAS-WATSONVILLE'` → `'SALINAS-WATSONVILLE CALIFORNIA'`), since the API can't prefix-match — three strings, no net lines. `deriveSeason`'s `startsWith` keeps working unchanged against exact names.

So yes, worth it. Two things about sequencing before I do it:

- It rewrites all 14 caches (~12.4 MB → ~6 MB), so the current 5-file commit should land first or it'll be buried.
- It costs 14 requests. Note `--force --limit=N` still has the bug where it doesn't advance between runs, so this wants a single `--force` pass (14 × 500 ms ≈ 7s), not batching.

Say when and I'll run it.

An `la` region would share `data/raw/mars/ca/grapes.jsonc` with sfbay. Today that's harmless — the cache is complete and region-blind, and each region narrows it at derive time. **Fetch-time filtering is what breaks it**: whichever region fetched last wins, and the other's derive either throws "nothing ships from…" or silently reads a cache missing its districts.

So you'd need the union, and it's more than the 3 lines I quoted:

```ts
// group MARS crops across ALL regions by cache path, unioning districts
const byCache = new Map<string, { crop: MarsCrop; districts: Set<string> }>();
for (const region of regionSources)
  for (const crop of region.crops) {
    if (crop.type !== 'mars') continue;
    const e = byCache.get(cachePath(crop)) ?? { crop, districts: new Set<string>() };
    for (const d of crop.districts) e.districts.add(d);
    byCache.set(cachePath(crop), e);
  }
```

~9 lines, plus ~5 for staleness — a cache whose recorded `districts` no longer cover the union must refetch, otherwise adding `la` wouldn't trigger one. That's what `CacheMeta.districts` earns its place for. **Total lands around 21 lines**, not the ~7 I told you, so it's right at your threshold rather than comfortably under.

One subtlety worth stating because it's easy to get wrong: the union must be computed over **all** regions even when you run `bun run fetch sfbay`. The region argument should select which caches to *consider*, never which districts to fetch — otherwise a one-region run quietly writes a cache the other region can't use.

Two ways to play it:

- **Do the union now** (~21 lines). Correct permanently, and `la` is then purely additive.
- **Ship the 3-line version plus a ~5-line guard** that throws when two crops map to one cache path with differing districts. Total ~8 lines, gets the 52% now, and converts the silent-clobber into a loud failure the day someone adds `la`. The union gets written when there's a real second consumer to test it against.

I'd take the second. The union is speculative today — I can't test it, since there's no second California region to test *with* — and the guard costs almost nothing while making the failure impossible to miss. The alternative is writing and committing logic whose only exercise is hypothetical.

That's a 3× pixel-level zoom into a plate's top-left corner — the falloff is smooth, with no diagonal crease. The faint pencil rectangle is the artist's own guideline on the original sheet, not an artifact.

**The corner seam** was a real bug in my alpha function: `min(dx, dy)` switches which axis governs exactly along the 45° diagonal, and that discontinuity *is* the line. Replaced with a rounded-rect distance — straight along the edges, a quarter-circle within `radius` of each corner, which is the small border-radius you asked for rather than an oval.

**The cast is gone**, and better than with `!important`. `src/styles.d.ts` augments React's `CSSProperties` to accept `--*` keys:

```ts
declare module 'react' {
  interface CSSProperties {
    [property: `--${string}`]: string | number | undefined;
  }
}
```

One declaration, no casts anywhere in the project now or later. That's the right fix because passing a computed value into CSS is normal — it was only awkward because React's types don't model custom properties, and `!important` would have papered over a typing gap rather than solving it.

**`PageSize` is now a named component** with a docblock explaining why the rule is emitted rather than written into the stylesheet. `Poster`'s return opens with `<PageSize width={w} height={h} unit={unit} />`, which reads as intent instead of inline machinery.

Assets grew 676 KB → 1.0 MB. The rounded corners preserve more soft gradient, which WebP's alpha channel spends bits on. Worth knowing if that matters; `-alpha_q` is the dial.

CI green — 59 tests, `@page` still correct at `8.5in 11in`.

Done. `ci` covers `tsc --noEmit`, oxlint, oxfmt --check and the test suite — all green.

The smell is gone, and verified gone: `grep` for any `?? true` / `default ??` across `data/` and `src/` returns nothing. The flag is now required on `MarsCrop` and `ManualCrop`, so TypeScript refuses a crop that doesn't state its own visibility.

The one design decision worth your eye — I changed the factories' return type:

```ts
// Deliberately short of a whole crop: `default` is left to the list below, so that whether a
// crop is on the poster is stated by the crop rather than inherited from the office that
// happens to report it.
type Reported = Omit<MarsCrop, 'default'>;

const fresno = (name: string, color: string, commodity = name): Reported => ({ … });
```

Without that, `fresno()` would have to supply a `default` to satisfy the type, and we'd be back to an inherited value — just relocated from `build.ts` into the factory. Returning an incomplete crop is what forces the declaration to the call site:

```ts
{ ...fresno('Persimmons', '#b4693d'), default: true },
// Melons are grown here and sold here, but they read as supermarket produce rather than as
// something to go to a market for, so they wait in the picker.
{ ...elCentro('Watermelons', '#c77580'), default: false },
```

`build.ts` is now a straight pass-through, and `ny.ts`'s hand-authored blueberries declare `default: true` alongside their other fields.

I left `ProduceSchema`'s `default: z.boolean()` required, per the reasoning we landed on — it's the backstop that makes it impossible for the build to emit an entry that never decided. `tests/regions.test.ts` parses the real generated files through `RegionSchema`, so that path is covered.

The generated diff is still **`default:` lines only** — no span moved, so the poster is byte-identical apart from the picker. 15 crops, 13 shown by default.

Ready for commit 1. Reminder that `cornSweet.jsonc` and `peppersBellType.jsonc` are still sitting untracked in `data/raw/mars/ca/` — keep them out of this one.
